Revision 32, after D308 (changes requested on 2026-11-21, the owner's decision of 2026-11-20 given in chat, after T17's ninth review, verify-193, and T04's fifty-eighth, verify-194; the owner had answered revision 31's questions as D302 to D305, and D306 approved revision 31). R29, R30, P5 and P18 change; their ids, forms and risk tags do not. verify-193 failed T17 on R29, P5 and P18. A native HTTP start of the MCP server reached Uvicorn's bind under a lock turned on after its app's start-up check. A LangChainTracer handed in with a run's callbacks stayed active under the lock. And an exact copy of the shipped rule R2 under another policy name, with R2 used as a sentinel in the case's descriptions and profile, put R2 in the prompt. D308 accepts these as outside the guarantee, to push development forward, and asks for two scope limits, keeping everything else in revision 31. Under R29 and P18: `cma mcp`'s native HTTP start reads the offline lock as it is asked for, and the app it serves reads the lock again at start-up and before every request, refusing start-up and answering every request with 503 once the lock is on; a lock turned on in the moment between that start-up check and Uvicorn binding its socket is outside R29, since the socket then serves nothing of the server. Under R30 and P5: a rule of a custom policy whose id, description, metric, operator, threshold, referral bands, fail action and missing-figure action are all the shipped rule's own is that shipped rule, and its id reaches the prompt so the memo can cite it, as T17's second review asked; telling where an identical rule came from beyond that comparison is outside P5, since such a rule carries no text the shipped policy does not. R29, R30, P5 and P18 now state both. Revision 32 also writes two answers to revision 31's questions that asked for this revision and of which D308 says nothing. D302 asked for D291 in R29 and P18, with the answers to the other T17 questions: a run is governed by the lock its